urces are A, B, C. Our new candidate must integrate these three. Perhaps we can combine them in a way that hasn't been tried yet.\n\nLet's look at the diff of previous best? Not provided directly, but we have its hypothesis and outcome. It combined SoA, single parallel region spanning all iterations with SIMD worksharing and aligned clauses, manual 4x unrolling preserving exact summation order, restrict pointers, double-buffer ping-pong fusing force+integration into one loop.\n\nSo that already integrates A (SoA + OpenMP static), B (unroll + aligned simd + restrict), C (double buffer). So our new candidate could be a variation: maybe use `schedule(static)` on the outer i-loop but with chunk size 1? Or use `#pragma omp for simd` instead of just `#pragma omp for`? The previous best used \"SIMD worksharing\" meaning likely `#pragma omp for simd`. That vectorizes across i. But maybe we can also add `aligned` clause to the inner loop? Already done.\n\nAnother idea: use a single parallel region and inside it, instead of having separate loops per iteration, we could have one big loop over all iterations and bodies combined? For example, flatten (iter,i) into a single index and compute each body's final state after 10 iterations? But that would require recomputing forces for each iteration sequentially because positions change; cannot parallelize across iterations due to dependency. So no.\n\nMaybe we can improve memory layout further: use `restrict` on all pointers, and also align allocations to cache line boundaries (e.g., posix_memalign). The previous best likely used malloc which may not be aligned to 16 bytes for SIMD; but with `aligned(px,...:16)` clause, the compiler assumes alignment. If actual allocation is not aligned, that could cause crashes or miscompilation? Actually OpenMP aligned clause tells compiler it can assume alignment; if not true, undefined behavior. So we must ensure allocations are aligned to 16 bytes (or more). The previous best might have used `m