ad nbodies, then allocate arrays and read each body's six floats sequentially. But baseline reads Body structs; we need to convert. We'll do as B did: read all data into a temp array of size n*6, then scatter.\n- Use restrict pointers in function signature for force+integration loop.\n- Single OpenMP parallel region spanning all iterations:\n```\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    float *restrict lpx = px; // but we need to swap buffers each iteration? Actually double buffer: cur and nxt arrays. We'll have two sets of SoA arrays: cur_px,cur_py,... and nxt_px,nxt_py,... Then after each iteration swap pointers.\n}\n```\nBut swapping pointers inside parallel region is tricky because all threads need to see the same pointer values. We can use shared variables for current pointers and next pointers; but they change per iteration. Since we are in a single parallel region, we can have an outer loop over iterations with `#pragma omp barrier`? Actually if we fuse force+integration into one loop per iteration, each thread computes its assigned i's forces from cur arrays and writes to nxt arrays (positions updated). Then after the loop, all threads must wait until all have finished writing before swapping pointers for next iteration. So we need a barrier between iterations. The previous best likely used `#pragma omp for` inside parallel region with implicit barrier at end of each worksharing construct? Actually if we use `#pragma omp for` without nowait, there is an implicit barrier after the loop. So structure:\n\n```\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    float *cpx=cur_px; ... // but these are shared variables; we need to update them per iteration.\n    for (int iter=0; iter<N_ITER; ++iter) {\n        #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n        for (int i=0;i<n;++i) { compute from cpx... write to nxt_px[i] etc. }\n        // implicit barrier here\n        // swap pointers: but only one thread should do it? Actually all threads need to see updated pointer values; we can have a critical section or use shared variables and let each thread update its local copy after barrier.\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut swapping pointers is tricky because cpx etc. are private per thread if declared inside parallel region. We could declare them as shared and modify under `#pragma omp single`? But then other threads need to see updated values for next iteration; they would read the shared variable at start of each iteration, but there's a race unless we have barrier after swap.\n\nSimpler: use two sets of arrays and alternate by index parity. For example, have arrays px0,py0,pz0,vx0,... and px1,py1,... Then in each iteration, read from set (iter%2) and write to set ((iter+1)%2). That avoids pointer swapping entirely; just use conditional indexing or precomputed pointers per iteration. But inside parallel region, we can have a loop over iterations with `#pragma omp for` on i, and at the end of each iteration there is an implicit barrier (since #pragma omp for has barrier by default). Then next iteration uses different arrays based on iter parity. That works.\n\nSo structure:\n\n```\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    for (int iter=0; iter<N_ITER; ++iter) {\n        float *rpx = (iter%2==0)? px0 : px1;\n        ...\n        float *wpx = (iter%2==0)? px1 : px0;\n        #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n        for (int i=0;i<n;++i) { ... }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut note: the `#pragma omp for` inside a parallel region will distribute iterations among threads; after it, there is an implicit barrier. So all threads finish writing to wpx before next iteration reads from rpx (which now points to previous write buffer). That's correct.\n\nHowever, we also need to initialize velocities? The force computation updates vx[i] += dt*fx; then position update uses new velocity: x_new = x_old + vx_new * DT. In baseline, they do body_force which updates vx in place, then separate loop updates positions using updated vx. So the order is: compute forces based on current positions and velocities? Actually forc